---
<Document title="Problem reports | Frostyard" description="What Frostyard problem reports send, what they never send, how long they are kept, and your rights." eyebrow="Privacy" heading="Problem reports">
  <p class="lede">Frostyard images and apps can send short problem reports, so we can find and fix faults. Reporting is off until you turn it on. This page says what a report holds, where it goes, how long we keep it, and how to have it deleted.</p>
  <p><strong>Status:</strong> ChairLift v0.15.0 and later sends reports on Snow, Snowfield and Sundog after you turn reporting on. Those images get it with the next image release. On Floe, snosi-health sends reports after an administrator turns reporting on.</p>

  <h2 id="what-reports-are-for">What reports are for</h2>
  <p>Reports tell us which updates fail, which services break, which Frostyard apps hit errors, and which versions are in use. Each kind of report and its purpose is in <a href="#what-is-sent">What is sent</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="turn-it-on-or-off">Turn reporting on or off</h2>
  <p>Nothing is sent until you turn reporting on. When you turn it off, sending stops at once. The controls are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Snow, Snowfield and Sundog:</strong> ChairLift's Problems page has a <em>Send problem reports</em> switch. On Snow and Snowfield, ChairLift's first-run setup can also ask you, with the switch off. Each user of the computer decides for themselves. In <em>Ask each time</em>, ChairLift shows you the exact report before it sends it. In <em>Send automatically</em>, it sends without asking, and its <em>Sent reports</em> list shows the 50 newest reports it sent.</li>
    <li><strong>Floe:</strong> an administrator runs <code>sudo snosi-health reporting on</code> or <code>off</code>. <code>snosi-health reporting status</code> shows the choice. <code>snosi-health send --dry-run</code> prints the exact reports without sending them.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Frostyard apps never send anything themselves. They write problems to the journal on your computer. ChairLift on a desktop, or snosi-health on Floe, is the only program that sends. A computer that does not run a Frostyard image sends nothing.</p>
  <p>If a new version of the report format adds a kind of report or a field, sending stops until you agree again.</p>

  <h2 id="what-is-sent">What is sent</h2>
  <p>Every report has the fields below, and then the fields of its kind. Every value is a fixed choice, a number in a range, a version, or a name from a fixed list. No field holds free text. The machine-readable format is <a href="/schemas/problem-report/v1.json"><code>/schemas/problem-report/v1.json</code></a>.</p>
  <ProblemReportTable />

  <h2 id="what-is-never-sent">What is never sent</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No machine ID, boot ID, account, user name, host name or cookie.</li>
    <li>No error message, panic message, log text, command line or environment.</li>
    <li>No path, name or content of your files, and no core dump. When crash reports start, a crash report will name only the program that crashed and its package. A Rust app problem names a source file of Frostyard's own code.</li>
    <li>No date or time finer than a week. The intake and GlitchTip record the time a report arrives.</li>
    <li>Nothing about services or extensions that you added yourself.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="your-ip-address">Your IP address</h2>
  <p>Your IP address is personal data. It reaches Cloudflare, which carries the report to <code>report.frostyard.org</code> for us as our processor, under its data processing agreement. The intake uses the address only to limit each address to 10 requests a minute, with a short-lived counter. It does not store, log or forward the address, and it stores no key made from it. The server that keeps reports never receives it: the proxy in front of that server removes the forwarding headers and keeps no log.</p>
  <p>Cloudflare's own security features can record the address of a request that they block or challenge, in Cloudflare's security events.</p>

  <h2 id="where-reports-go">Where reports go and how long we keep them</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Reports:</strong> the intake checks each report against the format, then forwards each problem report to GlitchTip, an error tracker on a server in the United States. The weekly count is not forwarded. GlitchTip keeps reports for 72 days. Its nightly cleanup and 7 days of backup snapshots can keep parts of a report up to about 88 days in all. Every report is deleted within 90 days. GlitchTip keeps each value it has seen in a report field, such as an image version or a program name, with no link to any report and with no end date.</li>
    <li><strong>Counts for the statistics:</strong> for each accepted report, the intake also writes 1 data point to Cloudflare Workers Analytics Engine. A data point holds only values from the report, such as the kind, the image and its version. It holds no <code>report_id</code> and no IP address. Cloudflare records the time it writes each point and keeps the points for about 3 months.</li>
    <li><strong>Weekly statistics:</strong> totals for each week, kept in Cloudflare Workers KV with no end date, and published.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="public-statistics">Public statistics</h2>
  <p>We publish weekly totals on the <a href="/stats/">statistics page</a>. A number from 1 to 4 is never published, and neither is a number that would let you work one out from the totals. We never publish single reports.</p>

  <h2 id="your-rights">Your rights</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consent.</strong> Consent is the legal basis for reports (GDPR Article 6(1)(a)). You can withdraw it at any time: turn reporting off.</li>
    <li><strong>See what was sent.</strong> ChairLift's <em>Sent reports</em> list shows the 50 newest reports it sent, each with its <code>report_id</code>. By default, ChairLift keeps the <code>report_id</code> of up to 500 sent reports in <code>~/.local/state/chairlift/reports.json</code>. On Floe, the list is <code>/var/lib/snosi-health/sent.jsonl</code>. When a send run gets through all its reports, Floe drops the entries older than 90 days. A run that stops early drops nothing, so an older entry can stay until the next complete run (<a href="https://github.com/frostyard/snosi/blob/main/docs/adr/0030-floe-sends-problem-reports-with-admin-consent.md">snosi ADR-0030</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Delete a report.</strong> Send us its <code>report_id</code> and we delete that report. GlitchTip stores each report under its <code>report_id</code>, so we can find it. Backup snapshots keep it at most 7 more days. A deleted report stays in the statistics data points and the weekly totals, which hold no <code>report_id</code>. Without the <code>report_id</code> we cannot tell which report is yours, because reports hold no identifier (GDPR Article 11).</li>
    <li><strong>Complain.</strong> You can complain to a data protection supervisory authority.</li>
    <li><strong>No automated decisions.</strong> Reports are not used to make any decision about you.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="who-is-responsible">Who is responsible</h2>
  <p>Controller: {controller}</p>
  <p>Contact for questions and deletion requests: <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a></p>

  <h2 id="transfers">Transfers outside the EU</h2>
  <p>Reports are kept in the United States. Cloudflare processes requests on its global network, under its data processing agreement and the EU–US Data Privacy Framework.</p>

  <h2 id="source">Source</h2>
  <p>Every part of reporting is open source.</p>
  <ul>{sources.map(([label, href, text]) => <li><a href={href}>{label}</a>: {text}</li>)}</ul>
  <p>The history of this page is in <a href="https://github.com/frostyard/frostyard-org/commits/main/src/pages/privacy/problem-reports.astro">frostyard/frostyard-org</a>.</p>
</Document>
